{"id":"top-100-latest-year","title":"The 100 highest-paid City employees in fiscal 2026","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"Who were the 100 highest-paid City employees in fiscal 2026, and how much of each person’s pay was regular salary, overtime and other pay?","finding":"In fiscal 2026 the 100 highest-paid people were paid $56.2 million between them. For 73 of the 100, overtime was the largest part of their pay; for 27 it was regular salary. They work in Police (58), Sheriff (16), Public Health (11), Fire Department (8), Retirement System (6) and 1 other department. The largest overtime figure is $522,802 (Frank Harrell, Sergeant 3 (class Q052), on 6,132 paid hours). The highest total, $1.5 million, belongs to a row whose regular salary alone is $1.1 million for the class Deputy Sheriff (class 8304): that is far above anything the class is paid and looks like a one-time entry such as back pay rather than a rate of pay. All qualifying rows are listed.","how":"City payroll file, fiscal 2026; the same counting as the year-by-year table. “Years in the top 100” counts fiscal years since 2017 in which the same name in the same department was in the top 100, and is given only for names rare enough to follow. The note marks a regular salary more than twice the top published yearly rate for the class. Titles are printed as the payroll prints them (Dep is deputy, Chf is chief, Asst is assistant, Sprv is supervisor, Ofc is officer, EMT is emergency medical technician). In this file a full-time year is 2,080 paid hours in every fiscal year from 2017 to 2025 and 2,160 in fiscal 2026 (27 fortnights of 80 hours rather than 26), so every yearly amount for fiscal 2026 is about 3.8% larger than a like-for-like year.","limits":"Being on this list is not a finding about anyone. Overtime is paid for hours worked, at rates set by labour agreements; the question it raises is about staffing and about how many hours one person can safely work, which the overtime pages take up. Other pay at the top includes payouts of unused leave on retirement, which is money already earned, and pay written into individual terms of employment. A regular salary far above the class rate in one year is usually back pay, a settlement or a correction; the Controller’s payroll records would say which. People are named because the City’s published payroll names them.","columns":[{"k":"person","t":"Name on the payroll","type":"person"},{"k":"job","t":"Job class","type":"text"},{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"salary","t":"Regular salary","type":"usd"},{"k":"overtime","t":"Overtime","type":"usd"},{"k":"other","t":"Other pay","type":"usd"},{"k":"total","t":"Total pay","type":"usd"},{"k":"hours","t":"Paid hours","type":"int"},{"k":"yearsInTop100","t":"Years in the top 100 since 2017","type":"int"},{"k":"note","t":"Note","type":"text"}],"rows":[{"person":"Scott Neu","job":"Deputy Sheriff (class 8304)","department":"Sheriff","salary":1138861,"overtime":217606,"other":132343,"total":1488810,"hours":3518,"yearsInTop100":1,"note":"Regular salary is 7.5 times the top published yearly rate for the class; the file does not say why, and a one-time entry such as back pay would look like this"},{"person":"Frank Harrell","job":"Sergeant 3 (class Q052)","department":"Police","salary":220100,"overtime":522802,"other":45012,"total":787915,"hours":6132,"yearsInTop100":null,"note":""},{"person":"Alison Romano","job":"Chf Executive & Investment Ofc (class 1119)","department":"Retirement System","salary":454815,"overtime":0,"other":304434,"total":759249,"hours":2160,"yearsInTop100":4,"note":""},{"person":"Brian Schaffer","job":"Police Officer 3 (class Q004)","department":"Police","salary":204763,"overtime":502940,"other":44270,"total":751973,"hours":7262,"yearsInTop100":null,"note":""},{"person":"Dennis Lai","job":"Sergeant 3 (class Q052)","department":"Police","salary":199046,"overtime":486793,"other":32689,"total":718528,"hours":5294,"yearsInTop100":null,"note":""},{"person":